{{fbdoc item="title" value="UINTEGER"}}----
Standard data type: 32-bit or 64-bit unsigned, same size as ##[[KeyPgSizeof|SizeOf]]([[KeyPgAny|Any]] [[KeyPgPtr|Ptr]])##.
Equivalent to ##[[KeyPgUnsigned|Unsigned]] [[KeyPgInteger|Integer]]##.

{{fbdoc item="syntax"}}##
	[[KeyPgDim|dim]] //variable// [[KeyPgAs|as]] **Uinteger**
	[[KeyPgDim|dim]] //variable// [[KeyPgAs|as]] **Uinteger**<//bits//>
##
{{fbdoc item="param"}}
	##//bits//##
		A numeric constant expression indicating the size in bits of unsigned integer desired. The values allowed are ##8##, ##16##, ##32##, ##64## or ##128##.

{{fbdoc item="desc"}}
	32-bit or 64-bit unsigned whole-number data type, depending on the platform.

	If an explicit bit size is given, the data type uses the requested unsigned integer width. **UInteger<128>** holds values from 0 through 2^128-1. The selected data type is ##[[KeyPgUbyte|UByte]]## for **UInteger<8>**, ##[[KeyPgUshort|UShort]]## for **UInteger<16>**, ##[[KeyPgUlong|ULong]]## for **UInteger<32>** and ##[[KeyPgUlongint|ULongInt]]## for **UInteger<64>**. **UInteger<128>** is a distinct 16-byte scalar type.

	LLVM emits native i128 arithmetic and bitwise operations; ABS and SGN use the two-word runtime helper. On 32-bit targets, LLVM also uses runtime helpers for conversions between floating-point values and 128-bit integers. GCC and Clang use native `__int128` on targets that support it, with ABS and SGN using that same helper. On 32-bit targets, they use a 16-byte aligned two-word type and lower arithmetic through the runtime. C interop must use that structure layout; a native `__int128` declaration is compatible only when the compiler supports it and uses a matching ABI.

	On 32-bit Windows, the GAS backend keeps the four-byte stack ABI for local variables. UDT field offsets still use 16-byte alignment, but C code receiving a pointer to such a local must accept its four-byte alignment. The runtime helpers accept unaligned storage.

	The native GAS and GAS64 backends lower arithmetic through the runtime library. GAS32 passes 128-bit arguments as 16-byte stack copies and returns values through a hidden result pointer, matching the i386 ABI for the aligned two-word C structure. GAS64 uses the SysV two-register ABI where available and stack arguments when those registers are exhausted. The GAS64 non-SysV path passes the 16-byte value through a caller-owned copy and returns through a hidden result pointer.

{{fbdoc item="ex"}}
{{fbdoc item="filename" value="examples/manual/datatype/uinteger.bas"}}%%(freebasic)
#ifdef __FB_64BIT__
	Dim x As UInteger = 0
	Dim y As UInteger = &HFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
	Print "UInteger Range = "; x; " to "; y
#else
	Dim x As UInteger = 0
	Dim y As UInteger = &HFFFFFFFF
	Print "UInteger Range = "; x; " to "; y
#endif%%

{{fbdoc item="lang"}}
	- Not available in the //[[CompilerOptlang|-lang qb]]// dialect unless referenced with the alias ##**""__Uinteger""**##.

{{fbdoc item="diff"}}
	- New to ""FreeBASIC""

